{
  "lesson_id": "c1_advanced_modal_verbs_can_be_able_to_may_might",
  "level": "C1",
  "title": "Advanced modal verbs: can / be able to, may / might",
  "description": "Can и be able to оба выражают ability, но be able to становится важным в других tenses/aspects и для конкретного успешного события в прошлом. May/might выражают possibility.",
  "theory": {
    "rule_id": "RULE-C1-4-RU",
    "lesson_num": 4,
    "title": "Правила: Advanced modal verbs: can / be able to, may / might",
    "level": "C1",
    "overview": "Can и be able to оба выражают ability, но be able to становится важным в других tenses/aspects и для конкретного успешного события в прошлом. May/might выражают possibility.",
    "main_rule": "Can = ability now. Be able to удобен в future/perfect forms. Could = general past ability. Was/were able to или managed to часто яснее для one specific successful past event. May/might = possibility.",
    "form": "can + V1. be able to + V1. could + V1. may/might + V1. may/might have + V3.",
    "when_why": "I can solve the problem. / We'll be able to access the system tomorrow. / We were able to solve the problem. / It may be true. / She might have forgotten.",
    "examples": "I can solve the problem. → Я могу решить проблему.\nWe'll be able to access the system tomorrow. → Завтра мы сможем получить доступ к системе.\nWe were able to solve the problem. → Нам удалось решить проблему.\nIt may be true. → Возможно, это правда.\nShe might have forgotten. → Возможно, она забыла.",
    "special_cases": "Might иногда предполагает slightly lower certainty than may, но разница не всегда строгая. Could не всегда лучший вариант для one-off successful past event.",
    "common_mistakes": "❌ I'll can access it tomorrow. → ✅ I'll be able to access it tomorrow.\n❌ We could solve the specific problem yesterday. → Often clearer: We were able to solve the problem.",
    "dont_confuse": "Can/could — modal. Be able to — flexible tense/aspect. May/might — possibility.",
    "quick_summary": "can → ability now.\nbe able to → flexible tense/aspect.\ncould → general past ability.\nwas able to/managed to → specific past success.\nmay/might → possibility."
  },
